    "row_label": "1847",
    "column_index": 4,
    "column_header": "Column 4",
    "raw_text": "Great famine. People fled for grain to other countries. Some of the people offered their services for food, while others lived by begging; some of them took refuge at Surat, where the Native Shaookars, and particularly the Dutch Merchants, supplied the people with rice free of charge, or by receiving a reasonable price, according as the people were indigent or otherwise. The then Nawab of Surat also helped the distressed poor to a considerable degree, and made arrangements by which the grain dealers were made to sell according to a certain nerrick a fixed quantity of grain to each individual.",
    "value_numeric": null,
    "unit": "",
    "is_null_or_leader": false,
    "arithmetic_verified": false
  },
