    "row_label": "1895",
    "column_index": 4,
    "column_header": "Column 4",
    "raw_text": "There was an insufficient fall of rain in general. In some eight or ten seacoast villages there was a complete failure of rain; so the people of these villages left their homes and settled themselves in foreign territories, while others who became indigent received the Tuccavee (advances) from Government, and were allowed reasonable remissions in the Jummabundy settlement, in order to enable them to settle themselves and perform their occupations in the then next rainy season.",
